ey': 'container.image.tag', 'type': 'string', 'value': '2.2.0-frontend-proxy'}, {'key': 'host.name', 'type': 'string', 'value': 'otel-collector-agent-z7xdg'}, {'key': 'k8s.cluster.uid', 'type': 'string', 'value': 'bcd5933a-ce7f-4f9a-9e53-34ae371b7423'}, {'key': 'k8s.container.name', 'type': 'string', 'value': 'frontend-proxy'}, {'key': 'k8s.deployment.name', 'type': 'string', 'value': 'frontend-proxy'}, {'key': 'k8s.namespace.name', 'type': 'string', 'value': 'astronomy-shop'}, {'key': 'k8s.node.name', 'type': 'string', 'value': 'node0.htliang2-agent.insai-pg0.apt.emulab.net'}, {'key': 'k8s.pod.name', 'type': 'string', 'value': 'frontend-proxy-84788b44b-pvhpm'}, {'key': 'k8s.pod.start_time', 'type': 'string', 'value': '2026-03-18T02:37:12Z'}, {'key': 'k8s.pod.uid', 'type': 'string', 'value': 'e81b224e-30ad-4447-83cb-891eb1d0827b'}, {'key': 'k8s.replicaset.name', 'type': 'string', 'value': 'frontend-proxy-84788b44b'}, {'key': 'k8s.replicaset.uid', 'type': 'string', 'value': '0633dae7-e8b7-4108-b9e1-2cdf2a6b1f07'}, {'key': 'os.type', 'type': 'string', 'value': 'linux'}, {'key': 'otel.library.name', 'type': 'string', 'value': 'envoy'}, {'key': 'otel.library.version', 'type': 'string', 'value': '5251fec52ee063192dcd8bd0615c6283e7b12b6c/1.34.12/Clean/RELEASE/BoringSSL'}, {'key': 'service.instance.id', 'type': 'string', 'value': 'astronomy-shop.frontend-proxy-84788b44b-pvhpm.frontend-proxy'}, {'key': 'service.namespace', 'type': 'string', 'value': 'otel-demo'}, {'key': 'service", id='3d2a7320-af95-4d95-9c2f-cad6f18fae52', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-963354451f63da7d'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='bb4d832d-5103-4f60-84e7-e48339907aa5'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue in the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application. So far, they've checked the services using get_services, looked at the pods with kubectl, checked metrics for errors, and retrieved traces for the frontend service. The traces showed some 308 status codes, which are Permanent Redirects, but overall, the HTTP status codes were 200 or 308. The user is now looking for the root cause.\n\nHmm, the next step should be to check if there's a service that's not responding correctly. The frontend service is the entry point, but maybe there's a misconfiguration in the routing. The traces show that the frontend is redirecting to frontend-proxy, which then calls the AdService. The 308 status might indicate that the frontend is redirecting to the wrong endpoint. \n\nWait, the user might need to check the actual service dependencies. Using get_dependency_graph could show how services are connected. If there's a misrouting or a service that's not properly configured, that could be the root cause. Also, checking the logs for the frontend-proxy or AdService might reveal more details. But since the user has access to the tools, maybe the dependency graph will show an incorrect link between frontend and AdService. \n\nAlternatively, checking the metrics for the AdService to see if there are any errors or latency issues. But the initial metrics query didn't show errors. Maybe the problem is in the routing configuration. So, using get_dependency_graph with a reasonable time frame could help visualize the service interactions and spot any anomalies. That's probably the next best step.\n</think>\n\nget_dependency_graph  \nThe traces indicate some 308 Permanent Redirects and internal calls to the AdService, suggesting potential misrouting or misconfigured service endpoints. Using `get_dependency_graph` will reveal the actual service interaction topology, exposing any incorrect dependencies, missing services, or abnormal traffic patterns that could explain the redirects and failed requests. This is critical for identifying architectural issues in the microservices communication.", additional_kwargs={