ked at the pods with kubectl, checked metrics for errors, and retrieved traces for the frontend service. The traces showed some 308 status codes, which are Permanent Redirects, but overall, the HTTP status codes were 200 or 308. The user is now looking for the root cause.\n\nHmm, the next step should be to check if there's a service that's not responding correctly. The frontend service is the entry point, but maybe there's a misconfiguration in the routing. The traces show that the frontend is redirecting to frontend-proxy, which then calls the AdService. The 308 status might indicate that the frontend is redirecting to the wrong endpoint. \n\nWait, the user might need to check the actual service dependencies. Using get_dependency_graph could show how services are connected. If there's a misrouting or a service that's not properly configured, that could be the root cause. Also, checking the logs for the frontend-proxy or AdService might reveal more details. But since the user has access to the tools, maybe the dependency graph will show an incorrect link between frontend and AdService. \n\nAlternatively, checking the metrics for the AdService to see if there are any errors or latency issues. But the initial metrics query didn't show errors. Maybe the problem is in the routing configuration. So, using get_dependency_graph with a reasonable time frame could help visualize the service interactions and spot any anomalies. That's probably the next best step.\n</think>\n\nget_dependency_graph  \nThe traces indicate some 308 Permanent Redirects and internal calls to the AdService, suggesting potential misrouting or misconfigured service endpoints. Using `get_dependency_graph` will reveal the actual service interaction topology, exposing any incorrect dependencies, missing services, or abnormal traffic patterns that could explain the redirects and failed requests. This is critical for identifying architectural issues in the microservices communication.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 425, 'prompt_tokens': 9998, 'total_tokens': 10423, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfed1-c29f-7512-bba0-5fabc5896b31-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 9998, 'output_tokens': 425, 'total_tokens': 10423}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='14f11def-9a90-4675-b2c8-4c5245359ec4'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is trying to diagnose an issue in the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application, which is a microservices architecture. They\'ve already checked the pods and found them all running, and they looked at some metrics and traces. The traces showed some 308 Permanent Redirects and calls to the AdService. Now, they want to check the dependency graph to see the service interactions.\n\nThe next step is to use the get_dependency_graph tool. The user mentioned that there are redirects and possible misconfigurations, so the dependency graph should show how services are connected. If there\'s a misrouting, the graph might show unexpected connections or missing services. The tool requires specifying the last_n_minutes parameter. Since the traces were checked for 10 minutes, maybe using the same time frame makes sense. But the user hasn\'t specified a time frame yet. Wait, in the previous step, they used get_traces with 10 minutes. The dependency graph tool also needs a time range. The user might want to use the same 10-minute window to correlate the data. So, the correct call would be get_dependency_graph with last_n_minutes=10. That should give the current dependencies, helping to see if there\'s an incorrect service reference causing the redirects. Let me make sure the parameters are correct. The tool\'s required parameter is last_n_minutes, an integer. So the call should be {"name": "get_dependency_graph", "arguments": {"last_n_minutes": 10}}. That\'s the right approach.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"last_n_minutes": 10}', 'name': 'get_dependency_graph'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-857c5cdb3915e553', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 354, 'prompt_tokens': 10984, 'total_tokens': 11338, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfed1-da07-79a3-8fb6-667efe2fadc7-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_dependency_graph', 'args': {'last_n_minutes': 10}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-857c5cdb3915e553', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 10984, 'output_tokens': 354, 'total_tokens': 11338}), ToolMessage(content='meta=None content=[TextContent(type=\'text\', text="{\'data\': [{\'parent\': \'frontend\', \'child\': \'cart\', \'callCount\': 30}, {\'parent\': \'checkout\', \'child\': \'product-catalog\', \'callCount\': 10}, {\'parent\': \'frontend-proxy\', \'child\': \'frontend\', \'callCount\': 59}, {\'parent\': \'payment\', \'child\': \'flagd\', \'callCount\': 1}, {\'parent\': \'frontend\', \'child\': \'recommendation\', \'callCount\': 1}, {\'parent\': \'frontend\', \'child\': \'ad\', \'callCount\': 8}, {\'parent\': \'checkout\', \'child\': \'payment\', \'callCount\': 4}, {\'parent\': \'frontend\', \'child\': \'product-catalog\', \'callCount\': 36}, {\'parent\': \'checkout\', \'child\': \'shipping\', \'callCount\': 10}, {\'parent\': \'load-generator\', \'child\': \'frontend-proxy\', \'callCount\': 106}, {\'parent\': \'recommendation\', \'child\': \'product-catalog\', \'callCount\': 4}, {\'parent\': \'frontend\', \'child\': \'product-reviews\', \'callCount\': 4}, {\'parent\': \'checkout\', \'child\': \'email\', \'callCount\': 5}, {\'parent\': \'cart\', \'child\': \'flagd\', \'callCount\': 5}, {\'parent\': \'checkout\', \'child\': \'cart\', \'callCount\': 8}, {\'parent\': \'shipping\', \'child\': \'quote\', \'callCount\': 5}, {\'parent\': \'checkout\', \'child\': \'currency\', \'callCount\': 14}, {\'parent\': \'frontend\', \'child\': \'checkout\', \'callCount\': 3}], \'total\': 0, \'limit\': 0, \'offset\': 0, \'errors\': None}", annotations=None)] isError=False', id='c3f348d4-9acb-4b28-aa99-1af24e15f039', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-857c5cdb3915e553'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='a0f2ced9-1b0e-4a48-adbd-ef1f549b64d2'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue in the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application. So far, they've checked the services, pods, metrics, traces, and dependency graph. The traces showed some 308 Permanent Redirects and calls to the AdService. The dependency graph shows frontend-proxy calling frontend, and frontend calling ad, which might be related.\n\nHmm, the 308 status code is a Permanent Redirect, which usually means the requested resource has been moved permanently. That could be due to a misconfigured endpoint or a routing issue. The dependency graph shows frontend calling ad, but maybe there's a problem with how the ad service is set up. \n\nWait, the user mentioned the application is a microservices architecture with OpenTelemetry instrumentation. So, maybe the issue is with the service discovery or the routing configuration. The frontend might be redirecting to the wrong service, like ad, but that service isn't handling the request properly, leading to the redirect loop. \n\nLooking at the traces, there's a span for 'GET /api/data' with a 308 status. The trace shows the request going through frontend-proxy to frontend, then to ad, and then maybe back to frontend. That could indicate a misconfigured route in the frontend service, causing it to redirect to the ad service instead of the correct one. \n\nThe next step would be to check the specific service configurations, like the routes in the frontend or frontend-proxy. Maybe using kubectl to describe the services or deployments to see the endpoints. Alternatively, checking the service mesh configuration if they're using something like Istio. But since the tools available include exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd, maybe they can check the service endpoints or pod logs. \n\nWait, the user already checked the pods and they're all running. So maybe the issue is with the service endpoints. Let me think. The frontend-proxy is part of the dependency graph, and it's calling frontend. If the frontend service isn't properly configured, maybe the endpoints are incorrect. Or perhaps the ad service isn't available, causing the redirect. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the frontend is misconfigured to redirect to the ad service instead of the correct service. The 308 suggests that the frontend is redirecting, but the ad service isn't handling the request, leading to the redirect. So the root cause could be a misconfigured route in the frontend service, causing it to redirect to the ad service instead of the intended service. \n\nTo confirm, the user could check the frontend service's configuration, like its deployment or service YAML files, to see if there's a misrouting. But since they can only use the provided tools, maybe they can use get_operations on the frontend service to see what operations are available, or check the logs of the frontend pod for more details. \n\nWait, the get_operations tool can be used to see the operations for a service. If they check the operations for the frontend service, they might see if there's an operation that's causing the redirect. Alternatively, checking the logs of the frontend pod using exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with logs command. \n\nBut the user hasn't used get_operations yet. Let me check the available tools again. The get_operations tool retrieves available operations for a specific service from Grafana. So using that on the frontend service might show the operations, which could indicate if there's an unexpected operation causing the redirect. \n\nAlternatively, since the dependency graph shows frontend calling ad, maybe the ad service isn't responding correctly, leading to the redirect. The user could check the ad service's status, but they already checked the pods and they're running. Maybe the ad service's endpoints are misconfigured. \n\nBut given the tools available, the next logical step is to check the operations for the frontend service to see if there's an operation that's causing the 308 redirect. So using get_operations on the frontend service would help identify if there's an operation that's not functioning as expected. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the issue is with the frontend-proxy service. The dependency graph shows frontend-proxy calling frontend, and the traces show requests going through frontend-proxy. If the frontend-proxy is misconfigured, it might be redirecting to the wrong service. \n\nBut the user has already checked the traces and dependency graph. The next step is to check the operations for the frontend service to see if there's an operation that's causing the redirect. So the tool choice would be get_operations with service set to frontend.\n</think>\n\nget_operations  \nThe dependency graph shows the **frontend** service calling **ad**, and traces indicate 308 Permanent Redirects. To understand what operations are being executed by the **frontend** service, I'll use `get_operations` to identify specific endpoints or functions that might be misconfigured, leading to redirects. This will help pinpoint whether the issue stems from an incorrect route, faulty service invocation, or improper HTTP handling in the frontend.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 1013, 'prompt_tokens': 10571, 'total_tokens': 11584, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfed1-ef80-72b0-96fe-724b6cbd8e4d-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 10571, 'output_tokens': 1013, 'total_tokens': 11584}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='6d8fd276-7bdc-4992-a82a-125281c0f07f'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue in the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application. So far, they've checked the services, pods, metrics, traces, and dependency graph. The traces showed some 308 Permanent Redirects, and the dependency graph shows the frontend service calling the ad service. Now, they want to check the operations of the frontend service to see what specific endpoints or functions are being called.\n\nThe next step is to use the get_operations tool. The frontend service's operations might reveal if there's a misconfigured endpoint causing the redirects. For example, maybe the frontend is trying to access a route that doesn't exist or is pointing to the wrong service. By checking the operations, they can see the exact endpoints and functions the frontend is invoking, which could help identify the root cause of the redirects. So, calling get_operations on the frontend service makes sense here.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"service": "frontend"}', 'name': 'get_operations'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8d5d11247ca6c6f7', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 210, 'prompt_tokens': 11565, 'total_tokens': 11775, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfed2-2171-7f70-af78-ff3796751fa9-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_operations', 'args': {'service': 'frontend'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8d5d11247ca6c6f7', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 11565, 'output_tokens': 210, 'total_tokens': 11775}), ToolMessage(content='meta=None content=[TextContent(type=\'text\', text="[{\'name\': \'oteldemo.CheckoutService/PlaceOrder\', \'spanKind\': \'client\'}, {\'name\': \'executing api route (pages) /api/product-reviews/[productId]/index\', \'spanKind\': \'internal\'}, {\'name\': \'POST /api/product-ask-ai-assistant/[productId]/index\', \'spanKind\': \'server\'}, {\'name\': \'POST\', \'spanKind\': \'server\'}, {\'name\': \'oteldemo.CartService/AddItem\', \'spanKind\': \'client\'}, {\'name\': \'oteldemo.CartService/GetCart\', \'spanKind\': \'client\'}, {\'name\': \'GET\', \'spanKind\': \'server\'}, {\'name\': \'oteldemo.AdService/GetAds\', \'spanKind\': \'client\'}, {\'name\': \'oteldemo.ProductReviewService/AskProductAIAssistant\', \'spanKind\': \'client\'}, {\'name\': \'GET /api/recommendations\', \'spanKind\': \'server\'}, {\'name\': \'oteldemo.ProductCatalogService/GetProduct\', \'spanKind\': \'client\'}, {\'name\': \'executing api route (pages) /api/cart\', \'spanKind\': \'internal\'}, {\'name\': \'POST /api/checkout\', \'spanKind\': \'server\'}, {\'name\': \'render route (pages) /\', \'spanKind\': \'internal\'}, {\'name\': \'GET /api/data\', \'spanKind\': \'server\'}, {\'name\': \'resolve page components\', \'spanKind\': \'internal\'}, {\'name\': \'GET /\', \'spanKind\': \'server\'}, {\'name\': \'execut