loyment name, then check the pods associated with that deployment. Alternatively, use "kubectl get pods -l app=ad -n astronomy-shop" to list all pods with the app label set to ad. That might give the correct pod name. So, the user should run "kubectl get deployments -n astronomy-shop" to find the deployment for the ad service, then get the pods from that deployment. Alternatively, use the label selector. Let me proceed with that.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nThe pod "ad-6c7d4665cc-h454d" was not found, suggesting it may have been deleted or renamed. To accurately retrieve logs, I will first identify the current pod name associated with the **ad** service using `kubectl get pods -l app=ad -n astronomy-shop`. This ensures I target the correct running pod, avoiding errors from stale or incorrect pod names.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': Usage(completion_tokens=959, prompt_tokens=12057, total_tokens=13016, completion_tokens_details=None, prompt_tokens_details=None), 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfed2-ccaf-7923-b5e3-dcf2f1be18c3-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 12057, 'output_tokens': 959, 'total_tokens': 13016}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='ac7c87ef-c220-476c-8883-c3b533e48b9e')]]
