oked at the pods with kubectl, checked metrics for errors, and retrieved traces for the frontend service. The traces showed some 308 status codes, which are Permanent Redirects, but overall, the HTTP status codes were 200 or 308. The user is now looking for the root cause.\n\nHmm, the next step should be to check if there's a service that's not responding correctly. The frontend service is the entry point, but maybe there's a misconfiguration in the routing. The traces show that the frontend is redirecting to frontend-proxy, which then calls the AdService. The 308 status might indicate that the frontend is redirecting to the wrong endpoint. \n\nWait, the user might need to check the actual service dependencies. Using get_dependency_graph could show how services are connected. If there's a misrouting or a service that's not properly configured, that could be the root cause. Also, checking the logs for the frontend-proxy or AdService might reveal more details. But since the user has access to the tools, maybe the dependency graph will show an incorrect link between frontend and AdService. \n\nAlternatively, checking the metrics for the AdService to see if there are any errors or latency issues. But the initial metrics query didn't show errors. Maybe the problem is in the routing configuration. So, using get_dependency_graph with a reasonable time frame could help visualize the service interactions and spot any anomalies. That's probably the next best step.\n</think>\n\nget_dependency_graph  \nThe traces indicate some 308 Permanent Redirects and internal calls to the AdService, suggesting potential misrouting or misconfigured service endpoints. Using `get_dependency_graph` will reveal the actual service interaction topology, exposing any incorrect dependencies, missing services, or abnormal traffic patterns that could explain the redirects and failed requests. This is critical for identifying architectural issues in the microservices communication.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 425, 'prompt_tokens': 9998, 'total_tokens': 10423, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfed1-c29f-7512-bba0-5fabc5896b31-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 9998, 'output_tokens': 425, 'total_tokens': 10423}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='14f11def-9a90-4675-b2c8-4c5245359ec4'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is trying to diagnose an issue in the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application, which is a microservices architecture. They\'ve already checked the pods and found them all running, and they looked at some metrics and traces. The traces showed some 308 Permanent Redirects and calls to the AdService. Now, they want to check the dependency graph to see the service interactions.\n\nThe next step is to use the get_dependency_graph tool. The user mentioned that there are redirects and possible misconfigurations, so the dependency graph should show how services are connected. If there\'s a misrouting, the graph might show unexpected connections or missing services. The tool requires specifying the last_n_minutes parameter. Since the traces were checked for 10 minutes, maybe using the same time frame makes sense. But the user hasn\'t specified a time frame yet. Wait, in the previous step, they used get_traces with 10 minutes. The dependency graph tool also needs a time range. The user might want to use the same 10-minute window to correlate the data. So, the correct call would be get_dependency_graph with last_n_minutes=10. That should give the current dependencies, helping to see if there\'s an incorrect service reference causing the redirects. Let me make sure the parameters are correct. The tool\'s required parameter is last_n_minutes, an integer. So the call should be {"name": "get_dependency_graph", "arguments": {"last_n_minutes": 10}}. That\'s the right approach.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"last_n_minutes": 10}', 'name': 'get_dependency_graph'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-857c5cdb3915e553', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 354, 'prompt_tokens': 10984, 'total_tokens': 11338, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfed1-da07-79a3-8fb6-667efe2fadc7-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_dependency_graph', 'args': {'last_n_minutes': 10}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-857c5cdb3915e553', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 10984, 'output_tokens': 354, 'total_tokens': 11338}), ToolMessage(content='meta=None content=[TextContent(type=\'text\', text="{\'data\': [{\'parent\': \'frontend\', \'child\': \'cart\', \'callCount\': 30}, {\'parent\': \'checkout\', \'child\': \'product-catalog\', \'callCount\': 10}, {\'parent\': \'frontend-proxy\', \'child\': \'frontend\', \'callCount\': 59}, {\'parent\': \'payment\', \'child\': \'flagd\', \'callCount\': 1}, {\'parent\': \'frontend\', \'child\': \'recommendation\', \'callCount\': 1}, {\'parent\': \'frontend\', \'child\': \'ad\', \'callCount\': 8}, {\'parent\': \'checkout\', \'child\': \'payment\', \'callCount\': 4}, {\'parent\': \'frontend\', \'child\': \'product-catalog\', \'callCount\': 36}, {\'parent\': \'checkout\', \'child\': \'shipping\', \'callCount\': 10}, {\'parent\': \'load-generator\', \'child\': \'frontend-proxy\', \'callCount\': 106}, {\'parent\': \'recommendation\', \'child\': \'product-catalog\', \'callCount\': 4}, {\'parent\': \'frontend\', \'child\': \'product-reviews\', \'callCount\': 4}, {\'parent\': \'checkout\', \'child\': \'email\', \'callCount\': 5}, {\'parent\': \'cart\', \'child\': \'flagd\', \'callCount\': 5}, {\'parent\': \'checkout\', \'child\': \'cart\', \'callCount\': 8}, {\'parent\': \'shipping\', \'child\': \'quote\', \'callCount\': 5}, {\'parent\': \'checkout\', \'child\': \'currency\', \'callCount\': 14}, {\'parent\': \'frontend\', \'child\': \'checkout\', \'callCount\': 3}], \'total\': 0, \'limit\': 0, \'offset\': 0, \'errors\': None}", annotations=None)] isError=False', id='c3f348d4-9acb-4b28-aa99-1af24e15f039', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-857c5cdb3915e553'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='a0f2ced9-1b0e-4a48-adbd-ef1f549b64d2'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue in the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application. So far, they've checked the services, pods, metrics, traces, and dependency graph. The traces showed some 308 Permanent Redirects and calls to the AdService. The dependency graph shows frontend-proxy calling frontend, and frontend calling ad, which might be related.\n\nHmm, the 308 status code is a Permanent Redirect, which usually means the requested resource has been moved permanently. That could be due to a misconfigured endpoint or a routing issue. The dependency graph shows frontend calling ad, but maybe there's a problem with how the ad service is set up. \n\nWait, the user mentioned the application is a microservices architecture with OpenTelemetry instrumentation. So, maybe the issue is with the service discovery or the routing configuration. The frontend might be redirecting to the wrong service, like ad, but that service isn't handling the request properly, leading to the redirect loop. \n\nLooking at the traces, there's a span for 'GET /api/data' with a 308 status. The trace shows the request going through frontend-proxy to frontend, then to ad, and then maybe back to frontend. That could indicate a misconfigured route in the frontend service, causing it to redirect to the ad service instead of the correct one. \n\nThe next step would be to check the specific service configurations, like the routes in the frontend or frontend-proxy. Maybe using kubectl to describe the services or deployments to see the endpoints. Alternatively, checking the service mesh configuration if they're using something like Istio. But since the tools available include exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd, maybe they can check the service endpoints or pod logs. \n\nWait, the user already checked the pods and they're all running. So maybe the issue is with the service endpoints. Let me think. The frontend-proxy is part of the dependency graph, and it's calling frontend. If the frontend service isn't properly configured, maybe the endpoints are incorrect. Or perhaps the ad service isn't available, causing the redirect. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the frontend is misconfigured to redirect to the ad service instead of the correct service. The 308 suggests that the frontend is redirecting, but the ad service isn't handling the request, leading to the redirect. So the root cause could be a misconfigured route in the frontend service, causing it to redirect to the ad service instead of the intended service. \n\nTo confirm, the user could check the frontend service's configuration, like its deployment or service YAML files, to see if there's a misrouting. But since they can only use the provided tools, maybe they can use get_operations on the frontend service to see what operations are available, or check the logs of the frontend pod for more details. \n\nWait, the get_operations tool can be used to see the operations for a service. If they check the operations for the frontend service, they might see if there's an operation that's causing the redirect. Alternatively, checking the logs of the frontend pod using exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with logs command. \n\nBut the user hasn't used get_operations yet. Let me check the available tools again. The get_operations tool retrieves available operations for a specific service from Grafana. So using that on the frontend service might show the operations, which could indicate if there's an unexpected operation causing the redirect. \n\nAlternatively, since the dependency graph shows frontend calling ad, maybe the ad service isn't responding correctly, leading to the redirect. The user could check the ad service's status, but they already checked the pods and they're running. Maybe the ad service's endpoints are misconfigured. \n\nBut given the tools available, the next logical step is to check the operations for the frontend service to see if there's an operation that's causing the 308 redirect. So using get_operations on the frontend service would help identify if there's an operation that's not functioning as expected. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the issue is with the frontend-proxy service. The dependency graph shows frontend-proxy calling frontend, and the traces show requests going through frontend-proxy. If the frontend-proxy is misconfigured, it might be redirecting to the wrong service. \n\nBut the user has already checked the traces and dependency graph. The next step is to check the operations for the frontend service to see if there's an operation that's causing the redirect. So the tool choice would be get_operations with service set to frontend.\n</think>\n\nget_operations  \nThe dependency graph shows the **frontend** service calling **ad**, and traces indicate 308 Permanent Redirects. To understand what operations are being executed by the **frontend** service, I'll use `get_operations` to identify specific endpoints or functions that might be misconfigured, leading to redirects. This will help pinpoint whether the issue stems from an incorrect route, faulty service invocation, or improper HTTP handling in the frontend.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 1013, 'prompt_tokens': 10571, 'total_tokens': 11584, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfed1-ef80-72b0-96fe-724b6cbd8e4d-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 10571, 'output_tokens': 1013, 'total_tokens': 11584}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='6d8fd276-7bdc-4992-a82a-125281c0f07f'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue in the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application. So far, they've checked the services, pods, metrics, traces, and dependency graph. The traces showed some 308 Permanent Redirects, and the dependency graph shows the frontend service calling the ad service. Now, they want to check the operations of the frontend service to see what specific endpoints or functions are being called.\n\nThe next step is to use the get_operations tool. The frontend service's operations might reveal if there's a misconfigured endpoint causing the redirects. For example, maybe the frontend is trying to access a route that doesn't exist or is pointing to the wrong service. By checking the operations, they can see the exact endpoints and functions the frontend is invoking, which could help identify the root cause of the redirects. So, calling get_operations on the frontend service makes sense here.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"service": "frontend"}', 'name': 'get_operations'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8d5d11247ca6c6f7', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 210, 'prompt_tokens': 11565, 'total_tokens': 11775, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfed2-2171-7f70-af78-ff3796751fa9-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_operations', 'args': {'service': 'frontend'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8d5d11247ca6c6f7', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 11565, 'output_tokens': 210, 'total_tokens': 11775}), ToolMessage(content='meta=None content=[TextContent(type=\'text\', text="[{\'name\': \'oteldemo.CheckoutService/PlaceOrder\', \'spanKind\': \'client\'}, {\'name\': \'executing api route (pages) /api/product-reviews/[productId]/index\', \'spanKind\': \'internal\'}, {\'name\': \'POST /api/product-ask-ai-assistant/[productId]/index\', \'spanKind\': \'server\'}, {\'name\': \'POST\', \'spanKind\': \'server\'}, {\'name\': \'oteldemo.CartService/AddItem\', \'spanKind\': \'client\'}, {\'name\': \'oteldemo.CartService/GetCart\', \'spanKind\': \'client\'}, {\'name\': \'GET\', \'spanKind\': \'server\'}, {\'name\': \'oteldemo.AdService/GetAds\', \'spanKind\': \'client\'}, {\'name\': \'oteldemo.ProductReviewService/AskProductAIAssistant\', \'spanKind\': \'client\'}, {\'name\': \'GET /api/recommendations\', \'spanKind\': \'server\'}, {\'name\': \'oteldemo.ProductCatalogService/GetProduct\', \'spanKind\': \'client\'}, {\'name\': \'executing api route (pages) /api/cart\', \'spanKind\': \'internal\'}, {\'name\': \'POST /api/checkout\', \'spanKind\': \'server\'}, {\'name\': \'render route (pages) /\', \'spanKind\': \'internal\'}, {\'name\': \'GET /api/data\', \'spanKind\': \'server\'}, {\'name\': \'resolve page components\', \'spanKind\': \'internal\'}, {\'name\': \'GET /\', \'spanKind\': \'server\'}, {\'name\': \'executing api route (pages) /api/products/[productId]/index\', \'spanKind\': \'internal\'}, {\'name\': \'executing api route (pages) /api/data\', \'spanKind\': \'internal\'}, {\'name\': \'GET /api/products/[productId]/index\', \'spanKind\': \'server\'}, {\'name\': \'executing api route (pages) /api/checkout\', \'spanKind\': \'internal\'}, {\'name\': \'GET /api/products/{productId}\', \'spanKind\': \'server\'}, {\'name\': \'oteldemo.ProductReviewService/GetProductReviews\', \'spanKind\': \'client\'}, {\'name\': \'GET /api/cart\', \'spanKind\': \'server\'}, {\'name\': \'executing api route (pages) /api/product-ask-ai-assistant/[productId]/index\', \'spanKind\': \'internal\'}, {\'name\': \'POST /api/cart\', \'spanKind\': \'server\'}, {\'name\': \'oteldemo.RecommendationService/ListRecommendations\', \'spanKind\': \'client\'}, {\'name\': \'executing api route (pages) /api/recommendations\', \'spanKind\': \'internal\'}, {\'name\': \'GET /api/product-reviews/[productId]/index\', \'spanKind\': \'server\'}]", annotations=None)] isError=False', id='c5152532-91a9-4eaf-97bc-0f84d9dbe01c', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-8d5d11247ca6c6f7'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='bacc7839-f073-4c4d-9252-32e171996875'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue in the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application. So far, they've checked the services, pods, metrics, traces, dependency graph, and operations. The traces showed some 308 Permanent Redirects, and the dependency graph indicated that the frontend is calling the ad service. The operations list includes calls to AdService/GetAds.\n\nHmm, the 308 status code is a Permanent Redirect, which usually means the requested resource has been moved permanently. That could be due to a misconfigured endpoint or a routing issue. The frontend is making calls to the ad service, and there's a redirect happening. Maybe the ad service isn't responding correctly, or the frontend is hitting the wrong endpoint.\n\nLooking at the operations for the frontend, there's an entry for 'oteldemo.AdService/GetAds' as a client operation. That's probably where the redirect is coming from. But why is there a redirect? Could the ad service be down? Or maybe the frontend is using an incorrect URL for the ad service. \n\nWait, the dependency graph shows the frontend calling the ad service. But if the ad service isn't available, the frontend might be getting a redirect. Alternatively, maybe the ad service is returning a redirect because the endpoint is misconfigured. \n\nI should check the status of the ad service. Are all its pods running? Let me use the kubectl command to check the pods for the ad service. If there are issues with the ad service's pods, that could cause the redirects. Also, checking the logs of the frontend and ad services might reveal more details about the redirect. But since the user can only use the provided tools, maybe they can check the pods and then look into the logs if possible. However, the available tools don't include logs directly. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the ad service's endpoints are not properly set up. The user could check the service configuration for the ad service. But the tools available are limited. The next step would be to check the pods related to the ad service to ensure they're running and healthy. If they are, maybe there's a configuration error in the service's DNS or endpoints. \n\nWait, the user already checked the pods in the namespace and saw that 'ad-6f9964ffd8-7jv9d' is running. So the pods are up. Then maybe the issue is with the service's configuration, like the target port or the selector. But without access to the service YAML, it's hard to tell. \n\nAlternatively, the frontend might be sending requests to the wrong service. For example, if the ad service is supposed to be at a different endpoint, but the frontend is hitting a different URL. The traces show the frontend calling 'ad' with a specific URL. Maybe the service name is incorrect, or the service is not properly exposed. \n\nSince the user can't directly check the service configuration, maybe they should look into the metrics for the ad service. If the ad service is not processing requests, that could cause redirects. But the earlier metrics query for HTTP errors didn't show any issues. \n\nAlternatively, the redirect could be due to a misconfiguration in the frontend's routing logic. For example, if the frontend is redirecting to a different endpoint that's not properly set up. The operations list shows that the frontend is calling 'ad' via 'oteldemo.AdService/GetAds', which might be the correct path. \n\nWait, the trace details show that the frontend is making a request to 'http://ad:8080' and getting a 308. Maybe the ad service is not listening on port 8080, or the service is not properly exposed. The user could check the service's YAML to see the port mappings. But again, with the available tools, they can't do that. \n\nAlternatively, the ad service might be a StatefulSet or has specific storage configurations, but the initial pod list shows it's a Deployment. \n\nSince the user has already checked the pods and they're running, the next step is to check the service's endpoints. But the available tools don't include checking service endpoints directly. The user might need to use the 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd' to get the service YAML or check the endpoints. \n\nWait, the user can use 'kubectl get service ad -n astronomy-shop' to check the service configuration. That would show the endpoints and ports. If the service is not pointing to the correct pods, that could cause issues. \n\nSo, the next tool call should be to check the service configuration for the ad service. But the available tools don't have a direct way to get the service YAML. However, the 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd' can be used with 'kubectl get service ad -n astronomy-shop' to retrieve the service details. That would help determine if the service is correctly configured to point to the ad pods. \n\nAlternatively, if the service is not properly set up, the frontend might be hitting the wrong endpoint, leading to a redirect. So checking the service's configuration is crucial here. \n\nTherefore, the next step is to use the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd to get the service details for 'ad' in the 'astronomy-shop' namespace. This will help verify if the service is correctly exposing the ad pods and if the ports are set up properly.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nThe traces and dependency graph indicate the **frontend** is calling the **ad** service, which is returning 308 Permanent Redirects. To verify if the **ad** service is misconfigured or unreachable, I will check its Kubernetes service definition using `kubectl get service ad -n astronomy-shop`. This will confirm if the service is properly exposing the correct pods and ports, which could explain the redirect issues.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 1207, 'prompt_tokens': 11345, 'total_tokens': 12552, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfed2-2d4e-7162-a217-a6e5bc0c7216-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 11345, 'output_tokens': 1207, 'total_tokens': 12552}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='f385c071-6ced-49c0-81c8-cdcdbad3b2b2'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application. They\'ve already checked the pods and found them all running. Then they looked at metrics and traces, which showed some 308 Permanent Redirects from the frontend to the ad service. The dependency graph confirmed that frontend is calling ad. Now, they used get_operations on the frontend and found that one of the operations is oteldemo.AdService/GetAds as a client.\n\nThe next step is to check the ad service\'s Kubernetes service definition. The user wants to make sure that the ad service is properly configured. So, they need to run kubectl get service ad -n astronomy-shop. This will show the service\'s endpoints and ports, ensuring that it\'s correctly pointing to the ad pods. If the service isn\'t properly set up, that could cause the redirects. So, the tool call should be exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command "kubectl get service ad -n astronomy-shop".\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get service ad -n astronomy-shop"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-ab1a843232f462a1', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 242, 'prompt_tokens': 12340, 'total_tokens': 12582, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfed2-6972-73f0-8953-db3c0243ff8d-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get service ad -n astronomy-shop'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-ab1a843232f462a1', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 12340, 'output_tokens': 242, 'total_tokens': 12582}), ToolMessage(content='NAME   TYPE        CLUSTER-IP       EXTERNAL-IP   PORT(S)    AGE\nad     ClusterIP   10.111.153.200   <none>        8080/TCP   5m4s\n', id='07f29b16-f1f7-4436-a81c-cb3d53594dc5', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-ab1a843232f462a1'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='10605a9d-6566-4f2a-8f38-6307208bed1b'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue in the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application. So far, they've checked the services, pods, metrics, traces, dependency graph, and operations. The main issue seems to be the 308 Permanent Redirects from the frontend to the ad service.\n\nFirst, they used get_services and found a list of services, but it was empty. Then they used kubectl to list pods in the namespace, which showed all pods are running. The metrics query for HTTP errors didn't return anything, so they checked traces for the frontend service. The traces showed some 308 redirects and calls to the ad service. The dependency graph confirmed that frontend calls ad. The operations for frontend included calls to AdService/GetAds. Then they checked the ad service's Kubernetes service, which exists as a ClusterIP with port 8080.\n\nNow, the next step is to check if the ad service is actually reachable. Since it's a ClusterIP, it's only accessible within the cluster. They need to verify if the ad service's pods are healthy and if there are any issues with the service's endpoints. Maybe the ad service isn't responding correctly, leading to redirects. They should check the pods of the ad service to see their status and logs. If the pods are not running or have errors, that could be the root cause. Also, checking the service's endpoints to ensure they're correctly pointing to the pods. If the endpoints are not set, the service might not be routing traffic properly, causing redirects. So, using kubectl to get the endpoints of the ad service would help confirm if the service is properly connected to its pods. If there are no endpoints, that's a problem. Alternatively, checking the logs of the ad service pods might show why they're returning 308 redirects, maybe a misconfiguration in the service's routing rules.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nThe `ad` service exists as a ClusterIP but its reachability and pod health need verification. I will check the service's endpoints and pod status using `kubectl get endpoints ad -n astronomy-shop` and `kubectl get pods -l app=ad -n astronomy-shop` to ensure the service is properly routing traffic to healthy pods. This will confirm if the 308 redirects stem from service misconfiguration or pod unavailability.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 493, 'prompt_tokens': 11584, 'total_tokens': 12077, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfed2-78b1-7f73-8164-248b56b132ae-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 11584, 'output_tokens': 493, 'total_tokens': 12077}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='fe3468f3-0b7a-44ac-aff5-87aa55037ec1'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application. They've already checked the pods and found them all running. Then they looked at metrics and traces, which showed some 308 Permanent Redirects from the frontend to the ad service. The dependency graph shows frontend calling ad, and the operations list includes calls to AdService/GetAds.\n\nThe next step was to check the ad service's Kubernetes service and endpoints. The service exists as a ClusterIP, but they need to ensure that the endpoints are correctly set up and that the pods are healthy. So, the user should run `kubectl get endpoints ad -n astronomy-shop` to check if there are any endpoints associated with the service. If there are no endpoints, that means the service isn't properly routing to the pods. Also, they should check the pods labeled with app=ad to make sure they're running and ready. If the pods are not in a Ready state or if there are no pods matching the label, that could explain the redirects. So, the next tool call should be to get the endpoints and pods for the ad service.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get endpoints ad -n astronomy-shop"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8e413040451618c8', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 271, 'prompt_tokens': 12584, 'total_tokens': 12855, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfed2-9159-7d52-a2e9-432934ec60cc-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get endpoints ad -n astronomy-shop'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8e413040451618c8', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 12584, 'output_tokens': 271, 'total_tokens': 12855}), ToolMessage(content='NAME   ENDPOINTS           AGE\nad     10.244.0.244:8080   5m13s\n', id='74509efd-b292-4f69-8b67-5908e9999167', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-8e413040451618c8'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='ae9b3251-d840-497e-9f2c-bc14d1061450'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop application. So far, they've checked the services, pods, metrics, traces, dependency graph, and operations. The main issue seems to be 308 Permanent Redirects from the frontend to the ad service.\n\nFirst, they checked the ad service's endpoints and found that it's pointing to 10.244.0.244:8080. The next step is to verify if that pod is healthy and running. They already checked the pods in the astronomy-shop namespace and saw that the ad-6c7d4665cc-h454d pod is running. But maybe they should check the specific pod's status in more detail.\n\nWait, the user already ran `kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop` and saw that the ad pod is running. But maybe they need to check the pod's logs to see if there are any errors or issues causing the redirects. The logs could show if the ad service is misconfigured, like incorrect routing or endpoints. Also, checking the pod's readiness and liveness probes would be important to ensure it's healthy.\n\nAlternatively, they could check the service's YAML configuration to see if the target ports or selectors are correct. But since the endpoints are correctly pointing to the pod, maybe the issue is with the pod itself. So, checking the logs would be the next logical step. If the logs show that the ad service is redirecting requests, that would confirm the issue is on the ad service's side. If not, maybe there's a network policy or ingress rule causing the redirect. But given the previous steps, checking the pod logs seems like the next best action.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nThe `ad` service has valid endpoints, but we need to confirm the underlying pod's health and logs. I will check the logs of the **ad** pod using `kubectl logs ad-6c7d4665cc-h454d -n astronomy-shop` to identify if there are misconfigurations, routing errors, or unexpected behavior causing the 308 Permanent Redirects. This will directly reveal issues within the service's implementation or environment.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 468, 'prompt_t